(docs/BUGS_FOUND.md #90, at its smallest. One call is enough: any single
 operation that pushes a buffer parameter past its current capacity moves the
 block and frees the old one. `Resize` and an out-of-capacity `Set byte` are
 the two spellings that can do it without a loop; both used to leave the
 caller holding freed memory, and the caller's next read segfaulted.)

To 'widen' with a buffer called sink.
    Resize sink to 9000.

To 'poke far out' with a buffer called sink and a number called where.
    Set byte where of sink to 65.

a buffer called widened is "start".
'widen' of widened.
Print "after resize: {widened's size}".
Print "and it still reads: {byte 1 of widened}".

a buffer called poked is "start".
'poke far out' of poked and 9000.
Print "after a far byte write: {poked's size}".
Print "the byte written: {byte 9000 of poked}".
